<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal Botanical Expedition to New Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal Botanical Expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> was a Spanish scientific enterprise directed by the priest and naturalist José Celestino Mutis from 1783 until his death in 1808 and continued until 1816. Based first at Mariquita and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, it described thousands of plants and produced more than 6,000 botanical illustrations, and it trained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> scientists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jose-de-caldas">Francisco José de Caldas</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Mutis had proposed the expedition in 1763 and again in 1764 without result. After he retired to Mariquita he met Antonio Caballero y Góngora, archbishop and viceroy, who put a third proposal to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> on his behalf; it was accepted in 1783, and the king named Mutis first botanist and astronomer of the expedition. Mutis had long corresponded with European scientists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a>, and in 1784 he was elected to the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The expedition was formally launched on 29 April 1783 with the botanists Eloy Valenzuela and Fray Diego García, the geographers Bruno Landete and Jose Camblor, a group of painters, collectors and a messenger. In 1790 it moved to Santafe de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>, where Mutis recruited Francisco Antonio Zea, his nephew Sinforoso Mutis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-jose-de-caldas">Francisco José de Caldas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-tadeo-lozano">Jorge Tadeo Lozano</a>, founded a free public School of Drawing and an Astronomical Observatory, and began classifying the flora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus">Linnaeus</a>'s principles for the Flora de Bogota the king had commissioned.</p><p>Smaller expeditions extended its reach. Caldas spent four years exploring what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and returned in 1808 with a large herbarium, and García explored the Upper Magdalena Valley. Alexander von Humboldt stayed with Mutis for over two months in 1801 and praised his collection. Mutis's method joined field collection to detailed descriptions of each plant's surroundings and its medical or industrial uses. He died on 11 September 1808, and Sinforoso Mutis led the expedition until 1816.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mutis's Arcanum of Quina, describing seven species with medical uses, appeared in a Santa Fe newspaper in 1793 and 1794. The plates, maps, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and more than 24,000 dried plants, with collections of woods, shells, resins, minerals and skins, were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and kept at the Royal Botanical Garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smithsonian">Smithsonian</a> curator E. P. Killip began studying the herbarium in 1932, and from 1952 the governments of Spain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> undertook the full publication of the Flora, a work expected to need more than sixty volumes and still unfinished.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal Botanical Expedition to New Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Royal Botanical Expedition of José Celestino Mutis</b> from 1783 trained a generation of Creole scientists.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a></li></ul></section>
